{"authored" [{"question" "q-83185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\frac{1}{x+3}$ about the point $x=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}$ and $a=-2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+3}=(x+3)^{-1} \\\\ f(-2) & =(-2+3)^{-1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}=-(x+3)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-2) & =-(-2+3)^{-2}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x+3)^{3}}=2(x+3)^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =2(-2+3)^{-3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\frac{6}{(x+3)^{4}}=-6(x+3)^{-4} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(-2) & =-6(-2+3)^{-4}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+3} & =1+(-1)(x+2)+\\frac{2}{2!}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(-6)}{3!}(x+2)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}-(x+2)^{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6e7db26c98ad76c4a61cac5b702e503c306953ea737723649dd3a34d84b9367" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1+2(x+2)+3(x+2)^{2}+6(x+2)^{3}+⋯"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}-(x+2)^{3}+⋯"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(x+2)^{3}}{6}+⋯"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(x+2)^{3}}{24}+⋯"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-\\frac{(x+2)}{2}+\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{6}-\\frac{(x+2)^{3}}{24}+⋯"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$ about $x=0$ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5).$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=0.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) \\\\ f(0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{0+1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =-\\frac{1}{(0+1)^{2}}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(0) & =\\frac{2}{(0+1)^{3}}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & =(0)+(1)(x-0)+\\frac{(-1)}{2!}(x-0)^{2}+\\frac{(2)}{3!}(x-0)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=0.5$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1.5 & ≈(0.5)-\\frac{1}{2}(0.5)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(0.5)^{3} \\\\  & =0.417\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6e7db26c98ad76c4a61cac5b702e503c306953ea737723649dd3a34d84b9367" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.412"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.415"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.417"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.421"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.424"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.417"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "a98457f22d3e827442c78ec589e34d4d3f62e4648befed21b8b62b5c1500029d" "basis" 1542 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-9623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=4$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{\\pi }.$ Give your answer to $4$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-9623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=4.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(4) & ={4}^{1/2}=2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(4) & =-\\frac{1}{4}(4)^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{{2}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =2+\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}(x-4)-\\frac{1}{2!}(\\frac{1}{{2}^{5}})(x-4)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =2+\\frac{x-4}{4}-\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{64}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\pi $ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{\\pi }.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\pi } & ≈2+\\frac{\\pi -4}{4}-\\frac{(\\pi -4)^{2}}{64} \\\\  & =1.773\\,9\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-9623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "855a329f75d6c9547335789135af70b608d40f74a828b15a6737ce119f20c439"} {"question" "q-9623" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-9623" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.732\\,2"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.764\\,9"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.773\\,9"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.787\\,7"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.792\\,7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-9623" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.773\\,9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\frac{1}{x+3}$ about the point $x=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}$ and $a=-2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+3}=(x+3)^{-1} \\\\ f(-2) & =(-2+3)^{-1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}=-(x+3)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-2) & =-(-2+3)^{-2}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x+3)^{3}}=2(x+3)^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =2(-2+3)^{-3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\frac{6}{(x+3)^{4}}=-6(x+3)^{-4} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(-2) & =-6(-2+3)^{-4}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+3} & =1+(-1)(x+2)+\\frac{2}{2!}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(-6)}{3!}(x+2)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}-(x+2)^{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "855a329f75d6c9547335789135af70b608d40f74a828b15a6737ce119f20c439"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1+2(x+2)+3(x+2)^{2}+6(x+2)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}-(x+2)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(x+2)^{3}}{6}+⋯"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(x+2)^{3}}{24}+⋯"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-\\frac{(x+2)}{2}+\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{6}-\\frac{(x+2)^{3}}{24}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}-(x+2)^{3}+⋯"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$ about $x=0$ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5).$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=0.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) \\\\ f(0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{0+1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =-\\frac{1}{(0+1)^{2}}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(0) & =\\frac{2}{(0+1)^{3}}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & =(0)+(1)(x-0)+\\frac{(-1)}{2!}(x-0)^{2}+\\frac{(2)}{3!}(x-0)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=0.5$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1.5 & ≈(0.5)-\\frac{1}{2}(0.5)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(0.5)^{3} \\\\  & =0.417\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "855a329f75d6c9547335789135af70b608d40f74a828b15a6737ce119f20c439"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.412"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.415"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.417"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.421"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.424"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.417"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-347637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ about $x=\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-347637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and $a=\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi )=-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi )=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{tan}^{2}⁡x+{sec}^{3}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi ){tan}^{2}⁡(\\pi )+{sec}^{3}⁡(\\pi )=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-1+(0)(x-\\pi )+\\frac{(-1)}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\pi +0.2$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2) & ≈-1-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\pi +0.2-\\pi )}^{2} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)^{2} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{25}) \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{50} \\\\  & =-\\frac{51}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-347637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "855a329f75d6c9547335789135af70b608d40f74a828b15a6737ce119f20c439"} {"question" "q-347637" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-347637" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1.02"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-353859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $x{e}^{x+2}$ about $x=-1.$\n\n$x{e}^{x+2}=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x+1)^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x+1)^{3}+⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-353859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=x{e}^{x+2}$ and $a=-1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x{e}^{x+2} \\\\ f(-1) & =(-1){e}^{1}=-e \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x+2}+x{e}^{x+2}=(1+x){e}^{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =(1-1){e}^{1}=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x+2}+(1+x){e}^{x+2}=(2+x){e}^{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(-1) & =(2-1){e}^{1}=e \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & ={e}^{x+2}+(2+x){e}^{x+2}=(3+x){e}^{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(-1) & =(3-1){e}^{1}=2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{x+2} & =-e+(0)(x+1)+\\frac{e}{2!}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{2e}{3!}(x+1)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =-e+\\frac{e}{2}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{e}{3}(x+1)^{3}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-353859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "855a329f75d6c9547335789135af70b608d40f74a828b15a6737ce119f20c439"} {"question" "q-353859" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-353859" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-e"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-353859" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-353859" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{e}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba04561458621a0c0660b43074943946b3ddd1"} {"question" "q-353859" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052556/state.json" "file_sha256" "fc477899cfc10a644cf65cc3a8ba0456145862